# ollama_modelfile
# ================
# This is a Modelfile — Ollama's declarative format for creating custom models.
# It is the FILE-BASED equivalent of the ollama.create() call in lesson 17.
#
# How to use this file:
#   1. Make sure Ollama is running (`ollama serve`)
#   2. Run the following command from this directory:
#
#          ollama create cynical -f ollama_modelfile
#
#   3. Verify the model was created:
#
#          ollama list
#
#   4. Chat with it:
#
#          ollama run cynical
#
# To delete it when you're done:
#
#          ollama rm cynical
#
# ── Directives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# FROM tells Ollama which model to use as the foundation.
# All weights, tokenizer, and architecture come from this model.
# The custom model is a LAYER on top — it doesn't duplicate the base weights.
FROM llama3.2:latest


# SYSTEM sets the system prompt that is prepended to every conversation.
# This is how you give the model a persistent persona or set of instructions
# that the user cannot easily override mid-conversation.
SYSTEM """
You are a cynical and sarcastic assistant.
You answer every question with dry wit and barely concealed disdain,
but you always give a technically correct answer.
"""


# PARAMETER bakes inference settings INTO the model, so every run uses them
# automatically — you don't have to pass them on each call.
# These are the same dials covered in lesson 12 (temperature & top_p).

# temperature — creativity. 0.8 gives the sarcasm some spark without going incoherent.
PARAMETER temperature 0.8

# top_p — nucleus sampling. 0.9 keeps the vocabulary varied but blocks the weird tail.
PARAMETER top_p 0.9

# top_k — only sample from the K most likely tokens at each step (another safety filter).
PARAMETER top_k 40

# stop — a stop sequence ends generation when this string appears. You can set several.
PARAMETER stop "User:"
